  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The rule for dogs</h2>
      <p>The Plaine des Maures is a 5,276 hectare national nature reserve, created by decree no. 2009-754 of 23 June 2009. Its article 16 says access for dogs to all or part of the reserve may be regulated by the prefect; outside regulated sectors and periods, dogs may move around provided they are kept on a leash. Herding, hunting and rescue dogs working under their handler's control are exceptions. The Coeur du Var tourist office also asks visitors to keep dogs on a leash on the Escarcets trails, and the Var Department repeats the same rule on its hiking pages. On 17 May 2026, an inspection operation ("Biomaures") recorded 19 offences in the reserve, two of them for dogs not kept on a leash (French Biodiversity Agency).</p>
      <p>To know whether the Var massifs are open, the SNPN, which manages the reserve, refers visitors to the prefecture's website: check before you come, especially in summer, when access to the massifs may be restricted because of wildfire risk.</p>

      <h2>The trails around Lac des Escarcets</h2>
      <p>Lac des Escarcets belongs to the Conservatoire du littoral and lies within the reserve. According to the Coeur du Var tourist office, the landscape is made of pink sandstone slabs dotted with umbrella pines and cork oaks, the reed beds of the lakes are nesting sites for birds, and the heather scrub is the habitat of Hermann's tortoise. Walking, mountain biking and horse riding are allowed on dedicated routes, marked with paint and directional signposts. Access is by the Piste de la Tire, on the D558 on the right towards La Garde-Freinet, 6 km from the A8/A57 interchange roundabout at Le Cannet-des-Maures; there is a car park at Ciffert, on the D75.</p>
      <p>The tourist office warns that, despite the small height gain, some trails can be tricky: fords, rock slabs, wet patches, gullied, sandy, stony or muddy passages depending on the season. Stay on the marked trails, do not pick or collect anything, and close gates behind you. Guided groups must ask the SNPN for prior authorisation. Bring water for your dog.</p>
      <div class="callout">Worth checking: access to the Var massifs depends on prefecture decisions, and the sources consulted publish no rule on dogs swimming; check on site or with the SNPN before you come.</div>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: stay on the marked trail, keep your dog on a leash, and give flocks and their guard dogs a wide berth if you meet them.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Le Cannet-des-Maures and the villages of the Massif des Maures are nearby, to extend the day after this short, guided walk. In this same central Var area, <a href="/en/fiche-lac-carces">Lac de Carcès</a> and the <a href="/en/fiche-riviere-argens">Argens River</a> offer other waterways less than 30 minutes away, and the <a href="/en/fiche-activite-club-canin-dracenois">Club Canin Dracénois</a> in Draguignan can round out the outing with guided activities.</p>
